---
// Page 13 — /blog/non-resident-mortgage-dubai/ · BLOG · Template C
// Research depth HIGH per spec: "current LTV caps and lender list change often".
// Which is exactly why this page states the structure and marks the numbers.
import PageShell from '../../../layouts/PageShell.astro';
import DataTable from '../../../components/DataTable.astro';
import FaqList from '../../../components/FaqList.astro';
import ValuationCrossLink from '../../../components/ValuationCrossLink.astro';
import NeedsSource from '../../../components/NeedsSource.astro';
import { byPath } from '../../../lib/pages.js';

const page = byPath('/blog/non-resident-mortgage-dubai/');

const answer =
  'Non-residents can borrow against Dubai property at a lower loan-to-value than residents, ' +
  'meaning a larger deposit. Fewer banks lend to non-residents at all, the documentation ' +
  'required from overseas is heavier, and the rate is usually higher. The current caps and ' +
  'the lending banks change often enough to check directly.';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Can a non-resident get a mortgage in Dubai?',
    a:
      'Yes, from a subset of UAE banks. The differences from a resident mortgage are a lower ' +
      'loan-to-value cap, a shorter list of lenders willing to consider the application, and ' +
      'more documentation to evidence income earned abroad.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What deposit does a non-resident need in Dubai?',
    a:
      'More than a resident, because the loan-to-value cap is lower. The exact figure is set ' +
      'by the lender within the regulatory cap and changes, so treat any number older than a ' +
      'few months as indicative and confirm it with the bank.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which banks lend to non-residents in Dubai?',
    a:
      'A subset of UAE banks, and the list changes as lending appetite shifts. Confirm ' +
      'directly rather than relying on a published list — a bank that lent to non-residents ' +
      'last year may not this year.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What documents does a non-resident need?',
    a:
      'Broadly: passport, proof of address, six to twelve months of bank statements, proof of ' +
      'income in its country of origin, and a credit report from your home country. Documents ' +
      'issued abroad often need attestation, which adds time.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are rates higher for non-residents?',
    a:
      'Usually, reflecting how lenders price the additional risk and the harder recovery ' +
      'position. Compare the total cost including arrangement and valuation fees rather than ' +
      'the headline rate alone.',
  },
];
---

<PageShell
  page={page}
  h1="Non-resident mortgages in Dubai"
  answer={answer}
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <h2>How does a non-resident mortgage differ from a resident one?</h2>

  <DataTable
    caption="Non-resident and resident borrowing compared"
    columns={['', 'Non-resident', 'UAE resident']}
    rows={[
      [
        'Loan-to-value cap',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: current non-resident LTV cap]</span>',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: current resident LTV cap]</span>',
      ],
      ['Deposit required', 'Higher, following the lower cap', 'Lower'],
      ['Lenders available', 'A subset of UAE banks', 'Most UAE banks'],
      ['Rate', 'Usually higher', 'Standard'],
      ['Documentation', 'Heavier, often requiring attestation', 'Standard UAE documents'],
      ['Income evidence', 'From the country where it is earned', 'UAE salary certificate'],
      [
        'Processing time',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: typical non-resident processing time]</span>',
        '<span class="pending">[NEEDS SOURCE: typical resident processing time]</span>',
      ],
    ]}
    source="Comparison of non-resident and resident lending terms in the UAE"
  />

  <p>
    The empty cells are the ones that change most often, which is precisely why they are marked
    rather than filled with a number from a year-old article. A borrower who budgets a deposit
    from a stale loan-to-value cap discovers the gap at the worst possible moment:{' '}
    <NeedsSource what="current UAE Central Bank LTV caps for non-residents and residents, plus the current list of lending banks, each with the date checked" />
  </p>

  <h2>What will a lender want to see?</h2>

  <ul class="two-col">
    <li>Passport and, where applicable, a second identity document</li>
    <li>Proof of address in your country of residence</li>
    <li>Six to twelve months of bank statements</li>
    <li>Proof of income — payslips, or accounts if self-employed</li>
    <li>A credit report from your home country</li>
    <li>Details of existing borrowing anywhere in the world</li>
    <li>The sale and purchase agreement for the property</li>
    <li>A valuation, which the lender will commission itself</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Documents issued outside the UAE frequently need attestation, and attestation is the step
    that adds weeks rather than days. Start it before you need it.
  </p>

  <h2>What does the lender’s valuation change?</h2>

  <p>
    Everything, if it comes in low. The bank lends a percentage of the valuation, not a
    percentage of the price you agreed. If the valuation lands below the purchase price, the
    shortfall is cash you must find, in addition to the deposit you had already planned for.
  </p>

  <p>
    That risk is higher in a softening market than a rising one, and this is a softening one:
    H1 2026 residential transactions in Dubai fell 13.6% year on year, and Q2 2026 saw both
    prices and rents decline. A price agreed against last year’s comparables can value
    differently against this year’s.
  </p>

  <h2>Who is this route realistic for?</h2>

  <p><strong>Workable if:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Your income is documented and verifiable in its country of origin</li>
    <li>You can fund the larger deposit the lower cap implies</li>
    <li>You have time for attestation and cross-border processing</li>
    <li>The property is a completed, conventional unit</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Difficult if:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Your income is irregular or hard to evidence abroad</li>
    <li>You have no credit history a UAE lender can read</li>
    <li>You need to complete quickly</li>
    <li>You are buying an unusual property type</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A broker earns their 0.5–1% most clearly on exactly this kind of application, because the
    binding constraint is knowing which lenders will look at your profile at all —{' '}
    <a href="/blog/mortgage-broker-dubai/">what Dubai mortgage brokers cost</a> covers when
    that is worth it and when it is not.
  </p>

  <ValuationCrossLink
    before="If you already own in Dubai and are considering borrowing against it rather than buying again, "
    anchor="a current written valuation"
    after=" is what any lender will start from, so it is worth having your own."
  />

  <FaqList faqs={faqs} />
</PageShell>
